[WARN ][o.c.o.WandsOpenSearchBenchTest] [testWandsBenchmark] Solr cross-check failed for fq=category_depth_1:"Rugs": java.security.AccessControlException: access denied ("java.net.SocketPermission" "localhost:8983" "connect,resolve")

Unlike the four blockers above and the four in P6E-E00's own log, this
one was NOT fixed in this pass -- disclosed here rather than silently
worked around. Running the benchmark as the non-root `ubuntu` user (the
fix for "can not run opensearch as root", see 02_*) also activates
OpenSearch's test-framework SecurityManager's network restrictions,
which deny outbound socket connections to Solr's port (localhost:8983)
from the test JVM. The identical live-Solr-count cross-check succeeded
without issue in P6E-E00's Elasticsearch benchmark, run as root -- so
this is specifically a consequence of the non-root + SecurityManager
combination OpenSearch's bootstrap requires, not a general network
policy restriction in this environment (outbound HTTP to Solr works
fine from every other process in this session, root or not).

Consequence: this run's `solr_count`/`count_match` columns are all
"n/a" -- no live, in-process correctness cross-check against Solr ran
for the OpenSearch numbers, unlike every other engine measured in this
research campaign. Partial, indirect corroboration is still available:
every candidate count OpenSearch reported (2,002 / 2,175 / 2,072 /
3,394 / 4,612 / 4,686 / 16,039 / 31,967) matches, digit-for-digit, the
Solr-verified ground truth counts from this same session's P6E-E00
Elasticsearch run for the identical 7 depth-1 checkpoints and the same
numeric-range query -- but this is a cross-process comparison against a
separately-verified number, not a live cross-check within this test's
own process. Named explicitly as an unresolved limitation in
PHASE6E_LOG.md/PHASE6E_DECISION.md, not silently treated as equivalent
to a live check.
